<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balkh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bactra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Balkh, known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as Bactra, is an ancient city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, near Mazar-i-Sharif, once the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>. It was a centre of Achaemenid, Greco-Bactrian, Kushan and early Islamic rule and a hub of Buddhist and Zoroastrian learning, later called the 'mother of cities'. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> sacked it in 1220, and it never recovered its former size.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balkh.jpg" alt="Balkh" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AhmadElhan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Green_Mosque,_Balkh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the west Mahmud defeated the Qarakhanids near <b>Balkh</b> in 1008, annexed Khwarazm in 1017 and took Rayy from the Buyids in 1029.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ghurids came to control the cities of Herat, Ghazni and <b>Balkh</b>, the trade routes between Khurasan and India and, from the 1190s, the rich farmland of the Punjab and the Ganges plain, whose treasuries and temples yielded enormous booty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Yet under their dominion Buddhism was predominant, with Zoroastrian and Manichaean minorities and many Hindu adherents; <b>Balkh</b> had some 100 monasteries and 30,000 monks, the capital of Chaghaniyan five, and the Buddhas of Bamiyan were begun under Hephthalite rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Bactria seceded from the Seleucid Empire under Diodotus I around 250 BCE; Euthydemus, a Magnesian Greek, seized its throne about 230 BCE and withstood a three-year siege of <b>Bactra</b> by Antiochus III, who in about 206 BCE recognised him and offered a daughter to his son Demetrius.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mahmud hurried back and, with war elephants and a disciplined professional army, defeated Nasr and Yusuf Qadir Khan near <b>Balkh</b> in January 1008.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Urban life flourished at Taxila, Peshawar, Mathura, Termez and <b>Bactra</b>, and irrigation works expanded farming in Bactria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
